      "id": "FAM:SF-APPA-061",
      "status_as_recorded": "untestable",
      "finding": "Comparison of deterministic vs. agent-based play-type recommendation agents yielded only 40 test pairs with 8 overrides; precision was 7.5% and no player-level overrides were scorable, making the question unanswerable with that data."
    },
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF-TMP-018",
      "status_as_recorded": "rejected",
      "finding": "Offenses do not consistently tilt play-type mix toward an opponent's prior defensive weakness; tilt -2.353 pp per PPP (t=-1.94) with a very small SD of weakness (0.017 PPP). Measurement reliability of play-type shares and game-plan adaptation was a limiting factor."
    }
  ],
  "failed_approaches": [
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF34",
      "what_failed": "Detecting a clear own-shot-mix adaptation to opponent's lack of a rim big",
      "why": "Effect sizes were small despite 1M possessions; rim-share change not significant and overall PPP null. Labelled WEAK/SMALL."
    },
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF26",
      "what_failed": "Predicting play-type changes after a hub substitution using structural influence features",
      "why": "Null result with adequate power, suggesting lineup-induced play-type shifts may be too noisy or confounded to detect with rich models."
    },
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF-TMP-018",
      "what_failed": "Showing that teams adapt play mix to opponent's prior defensive weakness",
      "why": "Effect in wrong direction and measurement reliability of play-type shares too low (SD of weakness 0.017 PPP)."
    }
  ],
  "evidence_establishes": [
    "Opponent lineup changes (e.g., rim big off) can produce measurable shifts in some shot-type frequencies (3PA, drives) even though overall efficiency may not change (SF34).",
    "Large sample sizes (~1M possessions) are available with NBA play-by-play data for studying shot-mix changes.",
    "Play-type-based analyses can suffer from low measurement reliability and small effect sizes, leading to null or inconclusive results (SF34, SF-TMP-018, SF26)."
  ],
  "evidence_does_not_establish": [
    "Whether changes in one's own offensive five produce larger or easier-to-detect shot-mix shifts than the opponent's lineup.",
    "Whether a within-game design that compares possessions immediately before and after a substitution can cleanly isolate personnel effects.",
    "The specific power and minimum detectable effect size for own-lineup changes using the available three-season dataset.",
    "That the proposed design is immune to regression-to-the-mean or confounding from simultaneous opponent substitutions."
  ],
  "own_inferences": [
    "Own-lineup changes are likely to have a larger influence on shot mix than opponent-lineup changes because they directly alter the shooting tendencies of the players on the floor.",
    "A within-game, fixed-effects design that holds opponent lineup constant and controls for period, clock, and score can provide a credible causal estimate.",
    "The detection goal is methodological ('showing the design can detect personnel effects'), so even a small but statistically significant shift in a composite shot-type distribution would satisfy the aim, provided all confounding is addressed.",
    "A pre-registered composite test (e.g., chi-squared or multinomial likelihood ratio) will avoid multiplicity problems while answering the core question."
